        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Real Sales", "{DL} says the sales were real.")],
        role: "Program investigator",
        exam: [.direct("What did your program audit find?", "\"Large, round purchases at odd hours, and swipes at {D}'s store with no product behind them. About $36,000 in food sales that never happened.\""), .direct("How long did this go on?", "\"The swipes began about six months before the audit. The terminal records show the pattern clearly.\""), .direct("What did your undercover visit show?", "\"Customers leaving with cash. The store camera shows people swiping benefit cards and walking out with money instead of food.\""), .direct("How does the program audit work?", "\"We analyze every store's transaction data for patterns that do not look like grocery shopping. We look at amounts, times, and how often card balances are drained.\""), .direct("What stood out about this store?", "\"Dozens of purchases for round amounts like $200 and $300, many at two or three in the morning. Real grocery sales rarely look like that.\""), .direct("What happened on your undercover visit?", "\"An investigator went in with a benefit card and was offered cash at half the balance. We saw the camera footage of others leaving with cash and no bags.\""), .cross("{DL} says the sales were real, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Some bulk sales are legitimate. But the amounts, timing, and lack of product don't fit.\""), .cross("Do some customers buy in bulk?", "\"Yes. A church or pantry might. I can't say every large sale was fake.\""), .cross("Did you check {DL}'s inventory?", "\"I reviewed what was available. I couldn't match the sales to the stock. That was part of the concern.\""), .cross("A few of the large purchases were from a church, weren't they?", "\"We identified a pantry that bought in bulk, yes. Those were a small fraction of the total.\""), .cross("The store has a large freezer room, doesn't it?", "\"Yes, and it held a limited stock. The volume sold on paper far exceeded what that room could hold.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Church pantry coordinator",
        defenseExam: [.direct("How do you know {D}?", "\"Our church pantry buys bulk food from {D}'s store, because it is the closest and the prices are fair. We have been customers for about three years.\""), .direct("What do you buy?", "\"Canned vegetables, rice, flour, and some frozen chicken for Saturday distribution. A volunteer drives over in a van and loads it up.\""), .direct("How large are your purchases?", "\"Between three hundred and six hundred dollars at a time, usually once or twice a month. We keep every receipt in a binder.\""), .direct("How do you pay?", "\"Sometimes by check and sometimes with a benefit-type card the church has for certain programs. The cashier rings it up like any other sale.\""), .direct("What have you seen of the store?", "\"Shelves stocked with canned goods, a meat counter, and a busy checkout lane. I have always seen real product go out the door with our purchases.\""), .direct("Have you ever seen anything improper?", "\"No. I have never seen cash handed out for cards, and I have never been offered such a thing.\""), .cross("Your purchases are nowhere near $36,000, are they?", "\"No. Over three years our total is a small fraction of that amount.\""), .cross("You shop only during the day, don't you?", "\"Yes, mornings or early afternoons, and we are done by noon. I have never been to the store late at night.\""), .cross("You cannot say what other customers do, can you?", "\"I cannot. I only know our own purchases and what I saw in the aisles.\""), .cross("You would not know if the register was used in other ways, would you?", "\"No, I would not. I can only speak to what our church bought.\""), .cross("You never checked the store's stock against its sales, did you?", "\"No. That is not something we would ever do. I only know that our orders were filled.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} cashed out benefits.", "Real sales."),
        allocution: "\"I made a mistake, and I'm sorry. I sold food at my store, and some customers bought a lot at once, including groups. I never paid cash on a card. I've worked hard to build my business, and I'm asking the court to understand the difference between bulk sales and what I'm accused of.\"",
        truths: ("{DL}'s inventory could not cover the sales.", "Some sales were bulk to a church."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you pay cash for benefit card balances?", "\"No. I sold food. Some customers bought a lot at once, including groups. I never paid cash on a card.\""), .direct("Why were there purchases at odd hours?", "\"Some customers shop late or early. Some sales were bulk orders for groups. I ran the sales when they came in.\""), .direct("What about the product?", "\"The food went out. I may have kept poor records. I know it looks like swipes without product, but the sales were real.\""), .direct("How did you build the business?", "\"I opened the store four years ago and added a meat counter and a bulk aisle. The neighborhood is poor and many customers use benefit cards.\""), .direct("What explains the odd-hour swipes?", "\"I run the store with long hours, and some of the customers work night shifts. I also did late swiping for people who needed to settle accounts.\""), .direct("What did you give customers?", "\"Some I gave extra groceries and a few I gave small cash advances. I thought of it as helping neighbors who were short.\""), .cross("Customers left with cash on your camera, didn't they?", "\"I understand how that looks. I may have given change back. I never paid half value for a card.\""), .cross("$36,000 is a lot in six months, isn't it?", "\"It is. My business picked up, and some orders were big. I can't explain every dollar.\""), .cross("You can't show inventory for those sales, can you?", "\"Not completely. My records were not perfect. I know that hurts.\""), .cross("Giving cash for a card balance is against program rules, isn't it?", "\"Yes, it is. I did it a few times, and I knew it was wrong.\""), .cross("Your inventory does not cover $36,000, does it?", "\"No. I bought some stock off the books and did not keep the receipts.\"")],
        openings: ("{D}, a store owner, billed a benefit program $36,000 in food sales that never happened, and an undercover visit showed customers leaving with cash instead of food. The State will present the audit, the terminal records, and the camera.", "The defense will present a store with real customers, including a church pantry that buys in bulk, and a records system that was messy but honest."),
        closings: ("Swipes without product, odd hours, round numbers, and customers leaving with cash. $36,000 in food sales that never happened is a fraud on the program.", "Some sales were bulk and real, and {D}'s record-keeping was poor. The State has not proved all $36,000 was fake or that {D.he} paid cash for card balances."),
        timeline: [(-180, "Swipes begin."), (0, "Month 6: the audit."), (0, "Month 6: undercover visit."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "An audit.",
        aggravating: ["The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let therapyBillingGhost = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.therapyBillingGhost",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Therapy Clinic Billed Medicaid for Sessions Never Held", "Patients Say They Never Went"],
        law: "insuranceFraud",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Presented false claims to an insurer, {V}, totaling $92,000, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "A therapy clinic run by {D} billed $92,000 for sessions that patients said never took place. Sign-in sheets were copied. {DL} says staff handled billing.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Claims", "Sessions billed on closed days."), E(.statement, 3, "Patients", "'I never went.'"), E(.document, 2, "Sign-In Sheets", "Copied handwriting.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Staff", "{DL} says staff handled billing.")],
        role: "Insurance investigator",
        exam: [.direct("What did your review of the clinic's claims show?", "\"Sessions billed on days when the clinic was closed. In all, the clinic billed $92,000 for sessions that patients said never happened.\""), .direct("What did patients tell you?", "\"'I never went.' Several patients told me they were never at those sessions.\""), .direct("What did you find about the sign-in sheets?", "\"The sign-in sheets had copied handwriting. The same writing appeared repeatedly, as if one person had filled them in.\""), .direct("How did you first notice something wrong?", "\"Our claims system flagged the clinic for a very high number of billed sessions per therapist. One therapist appeared to see twenty patients a day for months.\""), .direct("What did you check?", "\"I compared the claim dates against the clinic's published hours and found sessions billed on holidays and closed Fridays. I also called patients to ask if they attended.\""), .direct("What did the patients say?", "\"Most said they had never been to some of the billed dates, and a few said they had only attended a couple of times. Several said their signatures on sign-in sheets were not theirs.\""), .cross("{D} says staff handled the billing, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. {D} says that. But the claim forms were signed by {DL}.\""), .cross("A billing clerk made the claims, didn't the clerk?", "\"The clerk entered them, I understand. The clerk quit before the audit.\""), .cross("Did anyone see the defendant copy the sign-in sheets?", "\"No. I have no witness to that. I have the sheets and the pattern.\""), .cross("You never saw the defendant fill out a claim form, did you?", "\"No. I saw the forms with {DL}'s signature on them, but I cannot say who filled in the fields.\""), .cross("Some of the sessions were real, weren't they?", "\"Yes. A portion of the billed sessions did take place. The ghost sessions are the ones that fall on closed days or have patients denying them.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Billing clerk",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Who made the claims at the clinic?", "\"I did, as I was told. I entered the claims from the schedule I was given.\""), .direct("Who told you what to enter?", "\"{D}. {D.He} gave me the list of sessions and told me how to bill each one.\""), .direct("Why did you leave the clinic before the audit?", "\"I got a better job. I had no plan to dodge anything, and I did not think the billing was a problem.\""), .direct("How long did you work at the clinic?", "\"Two years as the billing clerk. I entered claims into the insurer's portal and sent them in.\""), .direct("Where did you get your information?", "\"From a weekly list that {D.he} gave me with patient names and dates. I typed in what the list said.\""), .direct("Did you ever ask about a date?", "\"Once, when a Friday showed up that I thought the clinic was closed. {D.He} said it was a makeup day, and I did not push it.\""), .cross("Did you ever check whether sessions took place?", "\"No. I did not. I entered what I was given and assumed it was accurate.\""), .cross("Did you enter claims for days when the clinic was closed?", "\"I may have. I did not check the calendar. I followed the list.\""), .cross("Who signed the claim forms?", "\"{DF} signed them. I entered the information and handed them over.\""), .cross("You never checked patient sign-in sheets, did you?", "\"No. I assumed they matched the list.\""), .cross("You left the clinic before the audit started, didn't you?", "\"Yes, about two months before, for a better paying job. I did not know an audit was coming.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} billed for ghost sessions.", "Staff billing."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry for everything this has caused patients and the program. Staff handled the billing, and I signed what was put in front of me. I relied on a clerk who left before the audit. I should have checked more carefully, and I'll cooperate with whatever the court asks.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} signed the claim forms.", "The billing clerk quit before the audit."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you bill for sessions that did not happen?", "\"No. I did not. Staff handled the billing, and I signed what was put in front of me.\""), .direct("Who handled the billing?", "\"The billing clerk. I relied on the clerk, who left before the audit.\""), .direct("Did you sign the claim forms?", "\"Yes. I signed them. I trusted the staff, and I did not check each one.\""), .direct("How did you run the clinic?", "\"I saw patients most of the day, four or five hours at a time, and left billing to the office. I signed forms at the end of each week.\""), .direct("What did you review before signing?", "\"A summary page with patient names and dates, which I skimmed. I did not look at each claim against the calendar.\""), .direct("What did you do when you learned about the audit?", "\"I hired a lawyer and sent the investigators everything I had. I told them my billing clerk had left a few months before.\""), .cross("$92,000 in claims for sessions that never happened. How did that go unnoticed?", "\"I was busy seeing patients. I did not review the billing, and I own that.\""), .cross("The sign-in sheets were copied. Who did that?", "\"I do not know. I did not copy them.\""), .cross("The clerk left before the audit. Did you tell the clerk to?", "\"No. The clerk left for a better job.\""), .cross("You signed every claim form, didn't you?", "\"Yes, every week for two years. I understand that is my signature.\""), .cross("The copied sign-in sheets were kept in your clinic, weren't they?", "\"They were in a filing cabinet in the front office. I did not copy them, and I do not know who did.\"")],
        openings: ("A therapy clinic run by {D} billed $92,000 for sessions that patients say never happened, on days when the clinic was closed. The sign-in sheets show copied handwriting, and {D.he} signed the claim forms.", "Listen for the defense evidence, which will show that staff handled the billing and that the billing clerk left before the audit. {D} was a clinician, not a bookkeeper."),
        closings: ("Claims on closed days, patients who never went, copied sign-in sheets, and forms signed by {D}. The clinic profited from sessions that did not exist.", "A busy clinician who relied on staff for billing, and a clerk who left before the audit. The State must prove {D} knew the claims were false."),
        timeline: [(-365, "Claims begin."), (0, "Year 1: audit."), (0, "Year 1: patients interviewed."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "Claim records.",
        aggravating: ["The offense was committed for pecuniary gain", "The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let liftingBackClaim = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.liftingBackClaim",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Worker Lifts Weights on Video While Claiming Back Injury", "Compensation Payments Reach $41,000"],
        law: "insuranceFraud",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Presented a false workers' compensation claim to an insurer, {V}, totaling $41,000, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} collected $41,000 in workers' compensation for a back injury while video showed {D.him} lifting weights and moving furniture on {DATE}. {DL} says it was therapy. The injury happened about six months before surveillance began. The claim file shows total disability, the doctor restricted {D.him} to ten pounds, and the video shows {DL} lifting heavy weights.",
        state: [E(.video, 3, "Surveillance", "{D} lifts heavy weights."), E(.document, 3, "Claim File", "Total disability claimed."), E(.statement, 2, "Doctor", "'Restricted to ten pounds.'")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Therapy", "{DL} says it was prescribed therapy.")],
        role: "Insurance investigator",
        exam: [.direct("What did the claim file show?", "\"A claim of total disability for a back injury, with $41,000 collected. The doctor restricted {D} to ten pounds.\""), .direct("What does your surveillance video show?", "\"It shows {DL} lifting heavy weights and moving furniture. The video is clear and shows real effort.\""), .direct("When did the surveillance happen?", "\"About six months after the injury. I filmed {D.him} on several days.\""), .direct("How was the injury reported?", "\"{D.He} reported a lower back injury from lifting a pallet at work and filed for total disability. The treating doctor restricted {D.him} to ten pounds with no bending.\""), .direct("How long did the payments run?", "\"About fourteen months, totaling $41,000 in wage replacement and medical costs. We opened a review when a tip said {D.he} was seen working on a car.\""), .direct("What did your investigator capture?", "\"Several days of footage, including {DL} loading plates onto a bar and benching what looks like more than a hundred pounds. On another day {DL} carries one end of a couch up a ramp.\""), .cross("Isn't lifting weights sometimes part of therapy?", "\"It can be. Light weights are. These did not look light.\""), .cross("{D} says {D.he} helped a friend with a couch, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. The video shows {DL} moving a couch with someone else.\""), .cross("Did the defendant look injured in the video?", "\"Not really. {D.He} moved normally. I could not see signs of pain.\""), .cross("A therapist can prescribe weights, can't they?", "\"Yes, in light amounts. The weights on the bar in the video are many times more than ten pounds.\""), .cross("You do not know what the defendant's pain was like that day, do you?", "\"No. I only know what the camera shows, and that is how {D.he} looked.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Physical therapist",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Did you prescribe weights for {D}?", "\"Yes, light ones. I prescribed light weights as part of recovery, to rebuild strength slowly.\""), .direct("Were the weights heavy?", "\"No. Not heavy. Nothing close to what the video shows, as far as I can tell.\""), .direct("How was the defendant's recovery going?", "\"Slowly. {D} had good days and bad days, and I did not expect heavy lifting.\""), .direct("What was the defendant's course of therapy?", "\"Six months of weekly sessions focused on stretching, core stability, and very light lifting. We started with two or three pounds.\""), .direct("Did you prescribe any heavy weights?", "\"No. The prescription capped at ten pounds, in line with the doctor's restriction.\""), .direct("What do you know about the defendant's injury?", "\"I saw tightness and muscle guarding in the exam, which fit a real strain. {D.He} winced during most movements.\""), .cross("Did you see the video?", "\"Yes. I saw it. It looks like more than I prescribed.\""), .cross("Could the defendant have lifted more than ten pounds?", "\"On a good day, perhaps. But it should not have been that much.\""), .cross("Did you know about the couch?", "\"No. I did not know until I saw the video.\""), .cross("The video shows far more than ten pounds, doesn't it?", "\"It does, and I was surprised by it. That is not what I prescribed.\""), .cross("You do not see the defendant outside of the clinic, do you?", "\"No, only in sessions. I cannot say what {D.he} did at home.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} faked disability.", "Therapy."),
        allocution: "\"I hurt my back at work, and I still have real pain every day. The lifting on that video was therapy, light weights to rebuild strength, as my therapist directed. I never meant to mislead anyone, and I'll explain it to the insurer however they wish.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} moved a couch on video.", "The therapist prescribed light weights."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you fake your injury?", "\"No. I did not fake it. I hurt my back at work, and I have real pain.\""), .direct("Why were you lifting weights?", "\"It was therapy. My therapist had me lifting light weights to rebuild strength.\""), .direct("What about the couch?", "\"I helped a friend move a couch. I thought it was okay, and I paid for it afterward.\""), .direct("How bad was your injury at first?", "\"I could barely get out of bed for the first month. I wore a brace and took pain medicine and lay flat most days.\""), .direct("What did you do at the gym?", "\"My therapist had me do light lifts to rebuild strength, and I tried to follow the plan. A few times I pushed too hard because I was tired of feeling weak.\""), .direct("Why did you help with the couch?", "\"A friend was moving, and I took one end because the friend was alone. I felt it that night and stayed in bed the next day.\""), .cross("The doctor restricted you to ten pounds, didn't the doctor?", "\"Yes, and I wrote it down on the form. I knew the limit, and I moved those boards anyway.\""), .cross("The video shows heavy weights, not light ones, doesn't it?", "\"It looks heavier than I remember.\""), .cross("You collected $41,000 while lifting. How is that honest?", "\"I was injured. The video shows good days, and I paid for the bad ones.\""), .cross("The video shows more than a light weight, doesn't it?", "\"It does look heavy, and I am not sure what I was lifting that day. I was having a good stretch.\""), .cross("You never told the doctor about the lifting, did you?", "\"No. I did not tell anyone about the heavier days. In hindsight, yes.\"")],
        openings: ("{D} collected $41,000 in workers' compensation for a back injury, claiming total disability and a ten-pound restriction. Surveillance video shows {DL} lifting heavy weights and moving a couch.", "The defense case is that the injury was real, that light weights were part of therapy, and that one good day on video does not make a fraud."),
        closings: ("Total disability claimed, a ten-pound restriction, and a video of {D} lifting heavy weights and moving furniture. {D.He} collected $41,000 on a false picture.", "A real back injury, a prescribed therapy, and a friend's couch. The State must prove {D} lied about the injury, and a good day on video is not that."),
        timeline: [(-180, "The injury."), (0, "Month 6: surveillance."), (0, "Month 6: the video."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "Surveillance video.",
        aggravating: ["The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let stormRepairAdvance = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.stormRepairAdvance",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Contractor Took Hurricane Repair Advances and Left Town", "Tarps Still on Roofs"],
        law: "falsePretenses100k",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Obtained over $100,000 by false pretenses from {V} and others, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "After a hurricane, {D}, a contractor, took $118,000 in advances from fourteen homeowners for roof repairs and left town. {DL} says materials were scarce. The storm came thirty days before the advances. {DL} collected them in the first week and was gone by the fourth, and no license was on record.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Advance Receipts", "$118,000 from fourteen homes."), E(.statement, 3, "Homeowner", "'He promised a week.'"), E(.document, 2, "License Check", "No license.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Scarcity", "{DL} says materials were scarce.")],
        role: "Homeowner",
        exam: [.direct("What did you pay {D} for?", "\"A roof repair after the hurricane. I paid an advance, and {D} promised the work within a week.\""), .direct("What happened after you paid?", "\"Nothing. {DL} did not show up, and I could not reach {DL}. {DL} was gone by the fourth week.\""), .direct("What did you learn about the defendant's license?", "\"No license was on record. I checked afterward. Fourteen homes paid advances totaling $118,000.\""), .direct("What was the damage to your home?", "\"The hurricane tore off about a third of my roof and let in rain over the kitchen and the bedroom. I had tarps up within a day.\""), .direct("How did you find the defendant?", "\"A flyer on my door a few days after the storm, and a recommendation from a neighbor. {D.He} came the next morning, walked the roof, and quoted $9,000.\""), .direct("What did the defendant ask for?", "\"Half up front, $4,500, to buy materials. I paid by check and was promised the work would start Monday.\""), .cross("Materials were scarce after the storm, weren't they?", "\"Yes. Shingles were hard to get. I will agree with that.\""), .cross("Did the defendant finish any roofs?", "\"I heard {DL} finished three. Mine was not one of them.\""), .cross("Did the defendant change phone numbers?", "\"Yes. The number I had stopped working.\""), .cross("You were one of fourteen who paid, weren't you?", "\"I learned later that fourteen households paid advances, and none of us knew about the others.\""), .cross("Many roofers were overwhelmed after the storm, weren't they?", "\"Yes, and I called a few myself. Most could not start for months, which is why I hired {D.him}.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Roof supplier",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Were shingles scarce after the hurricane?", "\"Yes. We had a shortage that lasted weeks. Everyone in town was waiting on orders, and contractors were calling us daily.\""), .direct("Did {D} order materials from your company?", "\"Some, yes. {D} ordered some shingles and picked up part of the order a few days after the storm.\""), .direct("Did the defendant pay for what was ordered?", "\"Partly. {D.He} paid for some of it and still owed us the rest when we last spoke.\""), .direct("What do you sell?", "\"Roofing shingles, underlayment, and flashing to contractors and homeowners. We were one of the few suppliers in the county after the storm.\""), .direct("What did the defendant order?", "\"About twelve squares of shingles and a stack of underlayment, enough for two or three roofs. {D.He} paid for some and left a balance.\""), .direct("How did the shortage affect you?", "\"We had a six-week backlog on certain shingles. Many contractors fought over every pallet.\""), .cross("Did the defendant order enough shingles to cover fourteen roofs?", "\"No, not nearly that many. The order was far smaller than fourteen roofs would need.\""), .cross("Did the defendant come back for the rest of the order?", "\"No. I never saw {D.him} again after that pickup, and the rest of the order stayed on our shelves.\""), .cross("Did the defendant tell you about advances taken from homeowners?", "\"No. I did not know anything about that. If I had, I would have asked some questions.\""), .cross("The order was small compared to what fourteen roofs need, wasn't it?", "\"Yes, far too small. Fourteen roofs would take more than ten times that amount.\""), .cross("{D} did not come back to finish the order, did {D.he}?", "\"No. The balance was never paid, and the shingles went to another buyer.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took advances.", "Scarce materials."),
        allocution: "\"I took those advances meaning to do the work once I could find materials, and I left to find supplies and planned to come back. I let it go too long, and I let those families down. They lost roofs in a storm and trusted me. I'll pay back what I can.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} changed phone numbers.", "{DL} did finish three roofs."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you plan to repair those roofs?", "\"Yes. I took the advances meaning to do the work, once I could get materials.\""), .direct("Why did you leave town?", "\"For supplies. Materials were scarce, and I went to find some. I planned to come back.\""), .direct("Did you finish any roofs?", "\"Yes. I finished three. I did what I could with what I had.\""), .direct("How did your business grow after the storm?", "\"Calls were nonstop, and I took on more than I could handle. I was one person with a truck and two part-time helpers.\""), .direct("What did you do with the advances?", "\"I paid for materials where I could, bought tools, and covered a deposit on a rental equipment truck. I ran short quickly when prices rose.\""), .direct("Why did you stop answering?", "\"I fell behind, got overwhelmed, and was too ashamed to face customers. I changed my number to get some peace.\""), .cross("You took $118,000 from fourteen homes and finished three roofs, didn't you?", "\"Yes. I know that is not enough. I was overwhelmed.\""), .cross("You changed your phone number, didn't you?", "\"I lost my phone. It was not on purpose.\""), .cross("You had no license, did you?", "\"No. I did not. I should have had one.\""), .cross("You took money from fourteen homes in the first week, didn't you?", "\"Yes, in about six days. I took more jobs than I could ever finish.\""), .cross("You did not have a contractor's license, did you?", "\"No. I had worked for others with one and thought I could do the work myself.\"")],
        openings: ("After a hurricane, {D}, a contractor, took $118,000 in advances from fourteen homeowners for roof repairs and left town within a month. No license was on record, and only three roofs were finished.", "Weigh the defense proof, which will show that materials were scarce, that {D} meant to complete the work, and that {D.he} finished three roofs before things fell apart."),
        closings: ("Fourteen homeowners, $118,000 in advances, no license, a changed phone number, and three roofs finished. {D} took money from storm victims and left.", "A contractor overwhelmed after a hurricane, with scarce materials and three roofs completed. The State must prove {D} never meant to do the work."),
        timeline: [(-30, "The storm."), (0, "Week 1: advances."), (0, "Week 4: {DL} vanishes."), (1, "Complaints.")],
        identified: "Receipts.",
        aggravating: ["The victim was particularly vulnerable", "The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        impact: "We slept under a tarp for weeks. The hurricane took the roof, and we gave that contractor our savings to fix it. Then {D.he} stopped answering. Rain kept coming through the ceiling, and we ended up borrowing from family. It's a hard thing to trust someone and lose everything twice.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"],
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let sickPuppiesPapers = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.sickPuppiesPapers",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Breeder Sold Sick Puppies With Fake Papers", "Six Families, Six Vet Bills"],
        law: "falsePretenses",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Obtained property of {V} and others by false pretenses, $6,200, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D} sold six puppies as registered purebreds with papers that were forged. Four were sick with parvovirus. {DL} says the registry made the error.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Registry Check", "No such registrations."), E(.statement, 3, "Vet", "'Parvovirus in four.'"), E(.document, 2, "Sale Receipts", "$6,200 total.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Registry", "{DL} says the registry made the error.")],
        role: "Buyer",
        exam: [.direct("What did you buy from {D}?", "\"A puppy that was sold as a registered purebred with papers. {D} sold six puppies in all for $6,200.\""), .direct("What did the registry check show?", "\"No such registrations. The papers were not real, and the numbers did not match any records.\""), .direct("What did the vet find?", "\"Parvovirus in four of the six puppies. Mine was one of the sick ones.\""), .direct("How did you find the puppy?", "\"I answered an online ad for a litter of registered purebred puppies, and drove an hour to see them. The seller showed me the parents and a folder of papers.\""), .direct("What did you pay?", "\"$1,100 in cash for a female puppy. The seller gave me a registration certificate and a vaccination card.\""), .direct("What happened after you took the puppy home?", "\"In three days the puppy stopped eating and began vomiting, and I rushed to the vet. The test came back positive for parvovirus.\""), .cross("{D} says the registry made an error, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"That is what {D.he} says. The registry later confirmed a clerical delay.\""), .cross("Did {D} use the same number twice?", "\"Yes. The same fake number appeared on more than one set of papers.\""), .cross("Were some of the puppies vaccinated?", "\"I was told some had shots, but not all.\""), .cross("The puppy had been checked over by a vet before you bought it, hadn't it?", "\"The seller said so and showed a card with a vet's name. I did not call to confirm.\""), .cross("You do not know whether the puppy caught the virus at the seller's home or at yours, do you?", "\"The vet said the timing pointed to the seller, since the incubation was a few days. I cannot prove it myself.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Registry clerk",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Does the registry sometimes have processing delays?", "\"Sometimes, yes. Registrations can take weeks to process, and papers can lag behind the actual litter by quite a while.\""), .direct("Was there a delay in this particular case?", "\"It is unclear. We have a record of a clerical delay around that time, but I cannot say it caused this.\""), .direct("Do breeders ever hand out papers before registration is final?", "\"Some do. It is not allowed, but it happens, and I have seen it with small breeders.\""), .direct("What is your role at the registry?", "\"I process breeder registrations and verify litter paperwork. I handle about a hundred filings a week.\""), .direct("What did you find when you checked this case?", "\"A litter registration was submitted but not yet processed, and the numbers on the sale papers did not appear in our system. The submission had sat in a queue for several weeks.\""), .direct("What is the typical processing time?", "\"Usually two to three weeks, but backlogs can push it to six. Breeders sometimes give out paperwork before approval.\""), .cross("Does a delay at the registry create a fake registration number?", "\"No. A delay means there is no number yet. It would not produce a false number on a form.\""), .cross("Did the number on these papers exist in your system?", "\"No, it did not exist in our system at all. We found no record under it.\""), .cross("Could someone simply have invented the number on the papers?", "\"Yes, it is possible. Anyone with a template could make a number that looks real.\""), .cross("A queue does not create a number, does it?", "\"No. A number is assigned only when we complete the file, and these numbers never existed.\""), .cross("Someone could make up numbers with a template, couldn't they?", "\"Yes, easily. That is what the papers look like to me.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} sold sick puppies.", "A registry error."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry for the families whose puppies got sick. I did not forge any papers. The registry made an error, and I relied on it. The puppies looked healthy to me, and I never knew about the parvovirus. I love dogs, and I'll refund every family.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} reused the same fake number.", "The registry confirms a clerical delay."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you forge the papers?", "\"No. I did not forge any papers. The registry made an error, and I relied on it.\""), .direct("Did you know the puppies were sick?", "\"No. They looked healthy to me. I did not know about the parvovirus.\""), .direct("Why was the same number on more than one paper?", "\"It was a mistake. I copied a number, and I did not notice.\""), .direct("How did you come to breed?", "\"My dog had a litter, and a friend offered to help sell them. I had never sold puppies before.\""), .direct("What did you tell buyers about papers?", "\"That they were registered and the papers would be included. I used a template a friend gave me with the breed club's logo.\""), .direct("What happened when you heard about the sick puppies?", "\"I called every buyer and offered to take them back. Two of the puppies had already been taken to a vet.\""), .cross("The registry says no such registrations exist, doesn't it?", "\"It does. I thought they were pending. I did not check.\""), .cross("Four of six puppies had parvovirus. Did you vaccinate them?", "\"Some of them. I tried to, but I did not finish all of them.\""), .cross("You reused the same fake number. How is that a mistake?", "\"I pasted it from a template.\""), .cross("You listed prices around $1,000 each, didn't you?", "\"Yes, about $1,000 to $1,200, and $6,200 for the six. I did not think twice about the amounts.\""), .cross("Did you ever check the registry?", "\"No. I never called, and I should have before I told anyone the numbers were pending.\"")],
        openings: ("{D} sold six puppies as registered purebreds with papers that turned out to be forged, and four of the puppies were sick with parvovirus. The registry has no such registrations, and the buyers paid $6,200.", "Jurors will hear that the registry had a clerical delay, that {D} reused a number by mistake, and that {D.he} did not know the puppies were sick."),
        closings: ("Six puppies, fake registrations, the same fake number twice, and four sick animals. Buyers paid for purebreds and got illness and paper.", "A clerical delay at the registry and a breeder who copied a number by mistake. The State must prove {D} meant to deceive buyers."),
        timeline: [(-30, "The litter."), (0, "The sales."), (5, "The puppies fall ill."), (14, "The check.")],
        identified: "A registry check.",
        aggravating: ["The offense was committed for pecuniary gain"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let executorEstateSales = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.executorEstateSales",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Executor Sold Estate Items and Kept the Cash", "Heirs Find Empty House"],
        law: "embezzlement",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Embezzled $48,000 belonging to {V}, an estate, by a person in a position of trust, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "{D}, the executor of an estate, sold furniture, tools, and a truck for $48,000 and kept the money. Heirs found an empty house. {DL} says estate debts used it.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Sale Records", "$48,000 in sales."), E(.document, 3, "Estate Accounting", "No deposits."), E(.statement, 2, "Heir", "'The truck was gone.'")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Debts", "{DL} says debts used the money.")],
        role: "Heir",
        exam: [.direct("What did you find when you went to the estate house?", "\"It was empty. The furniture, the tools, and the truck were all gone. I was one of the heirs.\""), .direct("What does the sale record show?", "\"$48,000 in sales of the furniture, tools, and a truck. {D} was the executor and made the sales.\""), .direct("What did the estate accounting show?", "\"No deposits. None of the $48,000 was ever put into the estate account.\""), .direct("What was in the house before?", "\"A full household, including an oak dining set, a workshop of hand and power tools, and a pickup truck in the driveway. The deceased had lived there for forty years.\""), .direct("When did you realize the property was gone?", "\"About six months after the death, when I drove by and saw an empty driveway and a for-rent sign. I asked the executor for an accounting and got excuses.\""), .direct("What did you do?", "\"I hired a lawyer and asked the clerk of court for the estate file. The file showed no inventory and no deposits.\""), .cross("{DL} says estate debts used the money, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Estates have debts. But I've seen no record of any debts paid from that money.\""), .cross("Was {DL} entitled to a fee?", "\"An executor can be paid a reasonable fee. I'm not aware of one being approved or accounted for.\""), .cross("Did you ask {DL} where the money went?", "\"Yes. I got vague answers. {D.He} said debts, but gave no list.\""), .cross("The will named {D} as executor, didn't it?", "\"Yes, I do not dispute that. {D.He} had authority to sell property to settle the estate.\""), .cross("You were not named for any of the specific items, were you?", "\"The will gave the residue to the heirs, including me. That is what makes the missing $48,000 matter.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Estate attorney",
        defenseExam: [.direct("What was your role in the estate?", "\"I am an attorney who advised {D} on opening the estate and the executor's duties. I handled the filings with the clerk.\""), .direct("What did you tell {D} about debts?", "\"That the executor must pay valid debts before distributing to heirs. We went through a list of bills, the funeral, hospital charges, and two cards.\""), .direct("What did you advise about selling property?", "\"That the executor could sell personal property to pay debts, but should deposit the proceeds in an estate account. I gave {DF} a written checklist.\""), .direct("What was the total of debts you saw?", "\"Roughly $22,000, including the funeral. That is the portion I reviewed and could verify.\""), .direct("Did the defendant follow your advice on deposits?", "\"Not fully. {DF} told me later that cash had gone straight to creditors, which I told {DF} was not the best way.\""), .direct("What happened when the heirs complained?", "\"I told {D.him} to prepare an accounting immediately. The accounting that came back was incomplete.\""), .cross("The debts were nowhere near $48,000, were they?", "\"No. The figure I saw was less than half of that.\""), .cross("You did not review every receipt, did you?", "\"I reviewed what {D.he} brought me, and it was partial.\""), .cross("You did not advise selling the whole house contents, did you?", "\"I advised selling some items. I did not expect everything to go.\""), .cross("An estate account should have been opened first, shouldn't it?", "\"Yes. That is standard, and I told {D.him} so.\""), .cross("You cannot say where the remaining money went, can you?", "\"No, I cannot. That is the gap that bothers me too.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} kept estate cash.", "Debts."),
        allocution: "\"The estate had debts that needed paying, and I sold the furniture and tools to raise the money, as an executor is supposed to. I used it for bills and expenses, but I didn't keep every receipt, and I should have. I know the family found an empty house, and that hurt.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} bought a boat.", "Funeral bills of $9,000 were paid from {DL}'s account."),
        defendant: [.direct("Why did you sell the furniture and truck?", "\"The estate had debts that needed to be paid. I sold the items to raise the money. That's what an executor does.\""), .direct("Where did the $48,000 go?", "\"Toward estate debts and expenses. I used it to pay bills. I didn't keep every receipt, and I should have, yes.\""), .direct("Did you take the money for yourself?", "\"No. I'd never steal from an estate. I was trying to settle everything, and I made a mess of the paperwork.\""), .direct("What debts did the estate have?", "\"Hospital and funeral bills, a medical collection, and two credit cards. The funeral alone was $9,000, and I paid it from my own account first.\""), .direct("How did you decide what to sell?", "\"I listed the furniture and tools with an estate sale company and sold the truck to a private buyer. I was told to turn property into cash quickly.\""), .direct("What did you do with the cash?", "\"I paid bills as they arrived and reimbursed myself for the funeral. I did not set up an estate account, which was a mistake.\""), .cross("You deposited nothing in the estate account, did you?", "\"No. I paid some bills directly in cash. I should have run it through the account.\""), .cross("The heirs found an empty house, didn't they?", "\"Yes. I sold everything. I thought the heirs would understand.\""), .cross("You can't show receipts for $48,000, can you?", "\"Not all of it. I know that's a problem. I'm trying to find what I can.\""), .cross("You bought a boat that year, didn't you?", "\"Yes, a used boat for about $11,000 with my own savings. It had nothing to do with the estate, but I see how it looks.\""), .cross("You never gave the heirs an accounting, did you?", "\"No. I kept meaning to, and I let it pile up.\"")],
        openings: ("{D}, the executor of an estate, sold furniture, tools, and a truck for $48,000. None of it reached the estate, and heirs found an empty house. The State will present an heir and the records.", "Jurors will also see an executor who faced real debts, sold property to pay them, and kept poor records. A sloppy executor is not a thief."),
        closings: ("$48,000 in sales, no deposits, and an empty house. {D} was trusted with the estate and the money never reached the heirs.", "The estate had real debts, and {D} sold property to meet them. The State has not proved {DL} kept the money rather than spent it, however poorly documented."),
        timeline: [(-90, "The estate opens."), (0, "Month 3: sales."), (0, "Month 6: heirs ask."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "Sale records.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .divided,
        impact: "{V.He} left us everything, and we came to the house to find it empty. The furniture, the tools, and my father's truck were all gone. {V.He} had worked {V.his} whole life for those things. No one told us they'd been sold. It felt like losing {V.him} all over again.",
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let tuitionCheckBookkeeper = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.tuitionCheckBookkeeper",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["School Bookkeeper Paid Personal Bills With Tuition Checks", "Parents' Checks Went to a Credit Card"],
        law: "embezzlement",
        threshold: 7,
        spec: "Embezzled $27,000 belonging to {V}, a private school, by a person in a position of trust, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "A private school's bookkeeper, {D}, deposited tuition checks into a personal account for a year, totaling $27,000. {DL} says it was a bank error. The deposits began a year before the audit, and the ledger marked each tuition payment as paid. The shortfall surfaced in the audit, when the deposit slips were found.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Deposit Slips", "Checks deposited to {D}'s account."), E(.document, 3, "Ledger", "Tuition marked paid."), E(.statement, 2, "Head of School", "The shortfall surfaced in audit.")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Bank Error", "{DL} says a bank error misrouted them.")],
        role: "Head of school",
        exam: [.direct("What did the audit reveal?", "\"That the school's bookkeeper, {D}, had deposited tuition checks into a personal account for a year, totaling $27,000.\""), .direct("What do the deposit slips show?", "\"Checks payable to the school deposited to {D.his} account. The deposits began a year before the audit.\""), .direct("What did the ledger say about those tuition payments?", "\"Each was marked paid. That is why the shortfall did not show up until the audit.\""), .direct("What was {D}'s job at the school?", "\"Bookkeeper for eleven years. {D.He} opened the mail, logged tuition checks in the ledger, and prepared the bank deposits.\""), .direct("How was the shortfall found?", "\"An outside auditor compared the ledger to bank statements and found twelve months of tuition deposits missing from our account. We then pulled the deposit slips from the bank.\""), .direct("How did you confirm whose handwriting was on the slips?", "\"I compared the writing on the backs to {D.his} handwriting on years of ledger entries and bank forms. It matched every time, and the auditor agreed.\""), .cross("{D} says a bank error caused it, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. The bank logged a routing problem once. I know that.\""), .cross("How many deposits went to the wrong account?", "\"Many, over a year. A single bank error would not explain it.\""), .cross("Did {D} pay a credit card with the money?", "\"The records suggest so. It looks like {D.he} paid a credit card with the money.\""), .cross("The ledger showed those families as paid, didn't it?", "\"Yes. The families received receipts, which is why none of the parents complained.\""), .cross("The bank did log a routing error once, didn't it?", "\"The bank told us about one error, and it was corrected within days. It affected a single deposit.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Bank officer",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Was there a routing error at the bank during that period?", "\"Once. We logged one routing problem during that time, and it was fixed within a few days of being found.\""), .direct("How was the routing problem fixed?", "\"We moved the funds back and corrected the routing instructions. It was a one-time issue that affected a handful of items.\""), .direct("Could one routing error explain a year of deposits?", "\"No, it could not. It was a single event, and a year of deposits would not come from that.\""), .direct("What is your role at the bank?", "\"I am a branch officer who reviews account activity and handles error reports. I have worked in banking for fourteen years.\""), .direct("What did your records show?", "\"One routing error during the period, on a single deposit that we corrected within a week. It was an input mistake at the teller window.\""), .direct("Do you review deposits?", "\"When an audit asks, yes. I looked at the deposits into {D}'s personal account and found checks made out to the school.\""), .cross("Did the error affect {D}'s account in any way?", "\"I do not recall that it did. I only know about one error, and I did not tie it to {D.him}.\""), .cross("Did you review the deposits into the defendant's personal account?", "\"Yes, later on. They were many, and they were not the kind of thing a routing error produces.\""), .cross("How often do bank errors repeat many times over a year?", "\"Almost never. That is why I would not call this an error, and I told the school that.\""), .cross("A teller would not deposit a school's check into a personal account without an instruction, would they?", "\"Not without a deposit slip naming the account. The slips gave the number.\""), .cross("Many routing errors do not happen in a year, do they?", "\"Almost never. One would be unusual, and a dozen would be a pattern.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} took tuition.", "A bank error."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry for what the school has been through. I did not take those checks. The bank logged a routing problem, and I believe it sent tuition deposits to my account by error. I've worked for this school for years. I'd ask the court to let the records speak for me.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} paid a credit card with the money.", "The bank logged a routing problem once."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you take the tuition checks?", "\"No. I did not take them. A bank error sent them to my account.\""), .direct("Was there really a bank error?", "\"Yes. The bank logged a routing problem. I believe it affected some deposits.\""), .direct("What about the credit card payment?", "\"A coincidence. I paid my card with my own money. I did not use tuition.\""), .direct("What did your job involve each week?", "\"I logged checks, prepared slips, and carried the deposit to the bank each Friday. I also managed payroll and vendor payments.\""), .direct("What did you believe about the deposits?", "\"I believed the bank had misrouted a few checks to my personal account because both were at the same branch. I did not check my statements.\""), .direct("How did you spend the money in your account?", "\"On ordinary bills and a credit card payment. I did not notice the additional balance because I did not look at the statements.\""), .cross("A year of deposits, and you did not notice?", "\"I did not look closely at my account. I trusted the bank.\""), .cross("The ledger marked each tuition payment paid. Who did that?", "\"I did. I was marking them as the checks came in.\""), .cross("$27,000 in your account that was not yours. You never asked why?", "\"I should have, yes. I did not look carefully, and I feel awful about it.\""), .cross("The slips have your handwriting on the back, don't they?", "\"Yes. I wrote on them as part of preparing the deposit.\""), .cross("Twelve months of deposits is not a single error, is it?", "\"No. A routing error would not repeat that many times, and I cannot explain it.\"")],
        openings: ("A private school's bookkeeper, {D}, deposited tuition checks into a personal account for a year, totaling $27,000, while marking each tuition payment paid in the ledger. The shortfall surfaced in an audit.", "By the end of this trial, the proof will show that the bank logged a routing error and that {D} did not notice deposits to a personal account. A bank mistake is not theft."),
        closings: ("A year of checks deposited to {D}'s account, a ledger marked paid, and a credit card paid with the money. The deposits were not accidents.", "A routing error logged by the bank and a bookkeeper who did not read a statement. The State must prove {D} knew and meant to take the money."),
        timeline: [(-365, "Deposits begin."), (0, "Year 1: audit."), (0, "Year 1: slips found."), (1, "{DL} is charged.")],
        identified: "Deposit slips.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"])

    static let unionTreasurerChecks = SmallCases.make(
        key: "story.unionTreasurerChecks",
        category: .whiteCollar,
        headlines: ["Union Treasurer Wrote Himself Checks From Dues Account", "$130,000 Over Four Years"],
        law: "embezzlement100k",
        threshold: 8,
        spec: "Embezzled over $100,000 belonging to {V}, a labor union, by a person in a position of trust, on or about {DATE}.",
        ages: 18...60,
        victim: true,
        summary: "A union treasurer, {D}, wrote himself checks totaling $130,000 over four years from a dues account. {DL} says they were approved expenses. The checks began four years before the audit. The bylaws allow no such payments, and a member says no one approved them.",
        state: [E(.document, 3, "Check Images", "Checks payable to {D}."), E(.document, 3, "Bylaws", "No such payments allowed."), E(.statement, 2, "Member", "'No one approved it.'")],
        defense: [E(.statement, 1, "Approved", "{DL} says the board approved expenses.")],
        role: "Union president",
        exam: [.direct("What did you find in the dues account?", "\"Checks totaling $130,000 payable to {D}, the treasurer, over four years. The check images show them.\""), .direct("What did a member tell you?", "\"That no one approved it. A member told me the board never signed off on those payments.\""), .direct("How did you become aware of the checks?", "\"A member asked to see the dues account summary at our annual meeting. When I pulled the statements, there were checks to {DL} nearly every month for four years.\""), .direct("What were the amounts?", "\"Typically $2,000 to $4,000 per check, adding to $130,000. The memo lines said expenses or reimbursement, with no detail.\""), .direct("What did the bylaws say about this?", "\"The treasurer may issue checks for union costs approved by the board. The bylaws never allow payments to the treasurer for personal expenses.\""), .direct("What did the audit show about when the checks started?", "\"The first check to the treasurer cleared four years before the audit, and similar ones followed every few months. The amounts grew year after year, and that pattern is what drew our attention.\""), .cross("{D} says they were approved expenses, doesn't {D.he}?", "\"Yes. {D.He} says so. Board minutes mention expense reimbursement.\""), .cross("Are there receipts?", "\"For some, yes. For about $90,000, no receipts exist.\""), .cross("Did the board approve some expenses?", "\"Some, yes. But not $130,000 worth.\""), .cross("The board minutes do mention reimbursement, don't they?", "\"They mention expense reimbursement in general terms. None of the minutes approve the amounts on the checks.\""), .cross("You did not review the account yourself for years, did you?", "\"No, I trusted the treasurer and the annual report. In hindsight that was a failure on my part.\"")],
        defenseRole: "Board member",
        defenseExam: [.direct("Did the board ever approve expenses for the treasurer?", "\"Some, yes. We approved certain reimbursements for union business, and the minutes reflect that approval at several meetings.\""), .direct("Did the board approve $130,000 in payments to the treasurer?", "\"No. Not that amount. I do not remember anything even close to that figure being discussed or approved.\""), .direct("How were those approvals recorded by the board?", "\"In the minutes. Some entries are vague, and they mention reimbursement without listing a particular amount or a receipt.\""), .direct("How long have you served on the board?", "\"Six years, and I voted on the budget each year. We met monthly and reviewed a short financial summary.\""), .direct("What did the minutes record about reimbursement?", "\"A line that said travel and organizing expenses would be reimbursed on submission of receipts. There was no total or limit.\""), .direct("What did you understand about {D}'s payments?", "\"That {D} was reimbursed for union travel. I never saw a list of checks.\""), .cross("Did you personally approve any of the specific checks?", "\"No. I did not approve specific checks. I voted on a general policy for reimbursing union expenses.\""), .cross("Were receipts supposed to be kept for those reimbursements?", "\"They should have been. I do not know why so many were missing, and I should have asked.\""), .cross("Did the treasurer need approval before writing checks to {D.himself}?", "\"For anything unusual, yes. The board should have been asked, and I do not recall that happening.\""), .cross("The board did not see individual checks, did it?", "\"No. The summaries did not break them down.\""), .cross("Nobody told the defendant to stop writing checks to {D.himself}, did they?", "\"No. We did not know the scale of it until the audit.\"")],
        theory: ("{D} paid himself.", "Approved expenses."),
        allocution: "\"I'm sorry for what the members are going through. Those checks were reimbursements for my work for the union, and I believed the board had approved them. They're in the minutes, though the language is vague. I've served this local for years and I'll account for every dollar.\"",
        truths: ("{DL} had no receipts for $90,000.", "Board minutes mention expense reimbursement."),
        defendant: [.direct("Did you pay yourself from the dues account?", "\"Yes, for expenses. They were approved reimbursements for my work for the union.\""), .direct("Who approved them?", "\"The board. They are in the minutes, though the language is vague.\""), .direct("Where are the receipts?", "\"I lost many of them. I kept a lot, but some were lost over the years.\""), .direct("What expenses did you incur?", "\"I traveled to regional conferences, negotiating sessions, and organizing visits across the state. I paid hotels, gas, meals, and printing from my own pocket.\""), .direct("How did you track what you spent?", "\"I kept receipts in a folder at first and later in a shoebox. After a move, many of them were lost.\""), .direct("What did the board know?", "\"I gave the board verbal reports and sometimes showed them the folder. Nobody asked me for more paperwork.\""), .cross("$90,000 with no receipts, isn't that right?", "\"Yes. I am not proud of it. I did not keep good records.\""), .cross("The bylaws allow no such payments, do they?", "\"They do not spell it out. I believed the board's approval was enough.\""), .cross("$130,000 in four years. Did you have that many expenses?", "\"I believed so. I traveled a lot for the union.\""), .cross("$90,000 of the checks have no receipts at all, do they?", "\"That is what the audit says. I cannot produce them, and I know that is a serious problem.\""), .cross("You signed every check yourself, didn't you?", "\"Yes, because I was the treasurer. I did not get a second signature.\"")],
        openings: ("A union treasurer, {D}, wrote {D.himself} checks totaling $130,000 over four years from a dues account. The bylaws allow no such payments, a member says no one approved them, and $90,000 has no receipts.", "The defense will present evidence that the checks were approved expenses mentioned in board minutes and that {D} was careless about paperwork, not dishonest."),
        closings: ("$130,000 in checks to the treasurer, no bylaw allowing it, no approval from members, and no receipts for $90,000. The dues belonged to the members.", "Board minutes that mention reimbursement and a treasurer who lost receipts. Sloppy records are not embezzlement, and the State must prove intent."),
        timeline: [(-1460, "Checks begin."), (0, "Year 4: audit."), (0, "Year 4: the bylaws."), (1, "{DL} is removed.")],
        identified: "Check images.",
        aggravating: ["The defendant took advantage of a position of trust or confidence"],
        mood: .wantsConviction,
        doctrines: ["burdenOfProof"],
        genders: ["D": .male])

    static let campaignTreasurerCar = SmallCases.make(
